## Lore

A ronin is a samurai without a master. Most become one when their daimyo is overthrown and slain, and the lord''s samurai lose office, income, home and often family with him. In a caste society the displaced warrior has no place: the new regime distrusts him, the people shun him, and other samurai and nobles look on him with disdain or fear. He is a living ghost, free of every tie but his own life, and that freedom makes him dangerous. His choices are ritual suicide, a hard search for a new master, or life as a sword for hire.

Some ronin leave service on their own, to spare their lord the shame their conduct might bring, or because they could not in conscience obey a cruel or unjust order; a few even strike down their old master. Either way they are treated alike. A ronin still carries the daisho, but cuts off the top-knot, shaves his head or wears another style entirely, and he may or may not keep to Bushido.

Many fall into poverty and turn bandit, thief, assassin or mercenary. Daimyo in other provinces hire them for missions no honorable samurai would take, or spend them in the front ranks. The stable New Empire produces few ronin, but the feuding kingdoms such as Otomo, Surusa and Yukimura produce many. Most drift to the Freelands and The Zone as mercenaries, adventurers or heroes; others wander the islands or go abroad, and a few serve the oni demon lords.

Alignments run to unprincipled, anarchist and evil; many ronin are bitter or disillusioned. About half drift from the samurai''s anti-technology creed and learn tech skills, which seals their fate as outcasts. Those who accept technology may use body armor, energy weapons, vibro-blades and vehicles, though most still trust the sword, the bow and the horse.

A ronin begins with only his horse and the weapons, armor and clothing he carries, and must earn or take everything else. A samurai who becomes a ronin keeps his level of experience.
', 'published', 'data-script'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'ronin');


-- Read the result back rather than trusting the exit code. d1-apply prints
-- these, and a CR in the stored markdown means the checkout mangled the file.
SELECT class_id, name, status, length(markdown) AS bytes, instr(markdown, char(13)) > 0 AS has_cr
  FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'ronin';

